=========================================================
   BLOCK-LEVEL CONTENT vs INLINE CONTENT
   ========================================================= 


---------------------------------------------------------
BLOCK-LEVEL CONTENT
--------------------------------------------------------- 

Definition:
Block-level content creates a rectangular block that starts
on a new line and occupies the full available width of its
parent container.

Characteristics:
- Always starts on a new line
- Takes full width by default
- Width and height can be applied
- Margin and padding work on all sides
- Used to structure and layout the page

Examples:
div, p, h1–h6, section, article

Mental model:
Each block-level element forms its own row.


---------------------------------------------------------
INLINE CONTENT
--------------------------------------------------------- 
Definition:
Inline content flows within a line of text and occupies only
the space required by its content.

Characteristics:
- Does NOT start on a new line
- Width and height cannot be applied
- Margin and padding work only horizontally
- Used for styling or highlighting text

Examples:
span, a, strong, em

Mental model:
Inline elements behave like words inside a sentence.



---------------------------------------------------------
KEY DIFFERENCE (SUMMARY)
---------------------------------------------------------

Block-level → layout structure, full-width rows
Inline      → text flow, content-sized

---------------------------------------------------------

=========================================================
   DISPLAY PROPERTY – block, inline, inline-block
========================================================= 


---------------------------------------------------------
1️⃣ display: block
---------------------------------------------------------

What is a block element?

- A block element always starts on a new line
- It takes the full width of its parent container

Key behaviors:
- Starts on a new line
- Takes 100% available width by default
- Width and height can be set
- Margin and padding work on all sides

Common block elements:
div, p, h1–h6, section, article

Example:
div {
    display: block;
    width: 200px;
    height: 100px;
}

Visual idea:
[ BLOCK ELEMENT (full width) ]
[ NEXT BLOCK ELEMENT        ]



---------------------------------------------------------
2️⃣ display: inline
---------------------------------------------------------
What is an inline element?

- An inline element stays inside a line of text
- It takes only as much width as its content

Key behaviors:
- Does NOT start on a new line
- Width is based on content only
- Width and height cannot be applied
- Padding and margin work only on left and right
- Top and bottom padding/margin have no effect

Common inline elements:
span, a, strong, em

Example:
span {
    display: inline;
    width: 200px;   /* ignored */
    height: 100px;  /* ignored */
}

Visual idea:
Text [INLINE] more text [INLINE]



---------------------------------------------------------
3️⃣ display: inline-block
--------------------------------------------------------- 

What is inline-block?

- inline-block is a mix of inline and block behavior

Key behaviors:
- Stays in the same line (like inline)
- Allows width and height (like block)
- Margin and padding work on all sides
- Does NOT take full width

Example:
div {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 150px;
    height: 100px;
}

Visual idea:
[ BOX ] [ BOX ] [ BOX ]

